findings.body:
The guard in `fetch_sdk_data` was changed from `full_name not in deployment_names` to `dep.name not in deployment_names`. When the user passes a short name such as `production`, `dep.name` is in the set, so the whole matching block is skipped. That means `short_name_matches` is never populated and the "Short deployment name ... matched N deployments" warning is never emitted.

I ran the suite. `tests/_sdk/test_fetcher.py::TestFetchSDKData::test_fetch_sdk_data_ambiguous_short_name_warning` passes on the base commit (34/34 in that file) and fails on the head commit (`assert any("Short deployment name 'production'" in w ...)` returns False). The full `tests/_sdk/` run is 326 passed and 1 failed.

The change is also unexplained. The PR description and docs don't mention it, and the existing full-name path was already correct. Reverting the line restores the original, passing behavior.

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="394-400"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The generated SDK contents don't change (both matching deployments are still included), but users lose the signal that their short name is ambiguous, and a test that guards this behavior now fails.

The async how-to example does `result = await future.result()`. On `PrefectFlowRunFuture`, `result()` is a synchronous method that wraps the async implementation. Awaiting its plain return value raises `TypeError: object ... can't be used in 'await' expression`. The async counterpart is `aresult()`.

This is the one usage the page presents as the async path, and users will copy it directly.

::code{file="docs/v3/advanced/generate-custom-sdk.mdx" lines="121-137"}

::suggestion{file="docs/v3/advanced/generate-custom-sdk.mdx" lines="132-132"}
    result = await future.aresult()

`logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)` was placed between `import prefect` and the `from prefect._sdk.models import ...` block. Every import after it is now flagged as E402 (module-level import not at top of file), and `ruff check` reports four errors (lines 21, 28, 35, 36). This will fail lint in CI.

The fix is to move the logger below the full import block, next to the existing module-level code. The standalone `import prefect` is also out of place, since the other imports are grouped together.

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="15-21"}

`_fetch_work_pools_parallel` used `zip(pool_names_list, results, strict=True)`, and the PR removed `strict=True`. Both lists come from the same set and `gather` returns one result per task, so the lengths can't diverge today. The guard's purpose is to fail loudly if they ever do. Without it, a future change that drops a result would silently skip work pools and their warnings.

Nothing in the PR explains the removal. Restoring it is the safer default.

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="218-222"}

`_fetch_work_pool` used to check `isinstance(variables, dict)` before assigning `job_vars_schema`. The PR drops that check and assigns `base_job_template["variables"]` directly. If the API ever returns a non-dict `variables`, that value flows into `WorkPoolInfo.job_variables_schema`, which is typed as a dict. The failure would then surface later, far from its source.

The PR doesn't say why the check is unnecessary. If the API guarantees a dict, a short comment would explain it. Otherwise, restoring the guard costs one line.

